From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 07:18:49 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 069/119] scene-referred S2: the engine-side switch and the
 convention latch

First item of Stage S2. Four unit decisions live inside Cry3DEngine, on the main thread, where
no renderer cvar can be read - and every one of them is cached until the next time-of-day
update. This item builds the mirror that carries the switch across that boundary and makes a
flip of it recompute everything that was cached, with nothing yet keyed on the new state.

Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s2-engine-side-switch.md, approach A. Research:
scene-notes/research/s2-pi-and-light-units.md section 5, s2-skybox-emittance-collision.md
section 5.

- E3DPARAM_SCENE_REFERRED, a new I3DEngine global parameter, stored in C3DEngine as a plain bool
  and read back through IsSceneReferredConvention(). This is exactly how time-of-day state
  already travels to the renderer (E3DPARAM_*), so nothing new is invented for it, and approach
  B (an e_SceneReferred cvar) was not needed. Approach C - moving the PI convention out of the
  3D engine entirely - stays recorded as the long-term direction: it is the cleanest
  architecture but m_vSunColor has 3DEngine consumers (fog, clouds, SVOGI injection, terrain)
  that would all need their own divides, and moving a divide is not guaranteed bit-identical.

- ccam publishes it from ApplySceneReferred(), next to the post-effect bus request, and restores
  it from RestoreSceneReferred() with the rest of the render state. The write goes through the
  new PublishSceneReferredConvention(), which does the two things the decision note asks for:
  it only acts ON A FLIP (comparing against the value already in the 3D engine), and on that
  flip it calls ITimeOfDay::Update(true, true). bForceUpdate is not cosmetic here - it is the
  call that reaches C3DEngine::SetSkyLightParameters(..., forceImmediateUpdate) and from there
  CSkyLightManager::FullUpdate(), so the Nishita sky is recomputed in one go instead of
  crossfading in at ~12 % per frame for eight frames. Without the re-push the sun would keep the
  old convention until the artist next moved the TOD slider. The flip is logged once.

- The renderer latches the convention beside the exposure.
  CD3D9Renderer::LatchSceneReferredExposure() now also writes m_fSceneReferredBRDFFactor[render
  thread] = sceneReferred ? 1/PI : 1.0, read back through GetSceneReferredBRDFFactor(). The
  hazard is real and is the reason the latch exists: the sun's colour is computed on the main
  thread while the render thread is a frame behind, so an unlatched flip would put the new sun
  in front of the old BRDF - one frame PI times too bright - or the reverse. One convention per
  frame, the same rule the exposure follows.

  Unlike the exposure, the convention is deliberately NOT forced to unity for SHDF_CUBEMAPGEN. A
  probe must be baked under the convention the scene is lit with; it is the exposure that must
  not be baked in, not the convention. That is what item 5's _rec suffix exists to record.

Nothing reads the new state yet - item 2 wires the sun, the clouds, the legacy migration and the
BRDF constant to it - so the picture is unchanged in both paths.

Built clean (0 errors, 0 warnings outside Code/SDKs): Cry3DEngine, CryRenderD3D11,
CinematicCamera.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus <noreply@anthropic.com>
